---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "@/components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/Footer.astro";
import Breadcrumb from "@/components/Breadcrumb.astro";

const leakReasons = [
  {
    title: "1. The 'Wicking Cuff' Effect (Long Grass & Trousers)",
    notes:
      "The #1 reason kids end up with wet socks isn't a hole in the boot—it's wet trouser hems and grass brushing against the fabric ankle collar. Moisture wicks down the inner lining like a sponge until socks are soaked."
  },
  {
    title: "2. 'Water-Resistant' vs. True Waterproof Membranes",
    notes:
      "Many high-street kids' boots only have a spray-on DWR coating over mesh or suede. DWR beads off light splashes, but pushing through soaking wet meadow grass forces water straight through the fabric within 15 minutes."
  },
  {
    title: "3. Worn-Out DWR & Heavy Mud Caking",
    notes:
      "Even Gore-Tex boots rely on an outer water-repellent finish. When mud dries onto the boot, it clogs the breathable pores and lets the outer fabric saturate, making the inside feel cold and clammy."
  },
  {
    title: "4. Foot Sweat Trapped Inside",
    notes:
      "Kids' feet sweat heavily when running. Without breathable membranes and merino socks, condensed sweat feels identical to a leak."
  }
];
---

<Layout
  title="Waterproof Membranes Explained: Why Kids' Boots Leak in Wet Grass | Peaks and Puddles"
>
  <Header />

  <main id="main-content" class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-12">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
      <!-- Automated Breadcrumb & Freshness Badge -->
      <div class="mb-6 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2">
        <Breadcrumb />
        <span
          class="bg-muted text-muted-foreground rounded-full px-3 py-1 text-xs font-medium"
        >
          Last checked: November 2026
        </span>
      </div>

      <!-- Article Header -->
      <header class="mb-10">
        <div class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
          <span
            class="bg-accent/15 text-accent rounded-full px-3 py-1 text-xs font-semibold"
          >
            Gear Explained
          </span>
          <span
            class="bg-muted text-muted-foreground rounded-full px-3 py-1 text-xs font-medium"
          >
            Footwear &amp; Socks
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
          Waterproof Membranes Explained: Gore-Tex vs. Proprietary Liners (And Why
          Kids' Boots Still Leak in Wet Grass)
        </h1>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed">
          Few things frustrate outdoor parents more than buying "waterproof"
          walking boots, only for your child to come back from a walk through
          wet grass with soaked socks. Here is how boot membranes actually work
          and the surprisingly simple reasons wet grass defeats them.
        </p>
      </header>

      <!-- Quick Summary Callout -->
      <div
        class="bg-card border-accent mb-12 rounded-xl border-l-4 p-6 shadow-sm sm:p-8"
      >
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">The Cheat Sheet for Busy Parents</h2>
        <ul class="text-muted-foreground mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">How membranes work:</strong>{" "}
            Waterproof boots contain an internal "bootie" liner (like Gore-Tex
            or ReimaTec) with microscopic pores too small for liquid water to
            enter, yet large enough for sweat vapor to escape.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Wet grass is tougher than rain:</strong>{" "}
            Walking through saturated grass acts like a continuous car-wash
            brush against the tongue and ankle cuff, wicking water over the top.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">The easy fix:</strong> Pull{" "}
            <a
              href="/gear-explained/waterproof-ratings-explained"
              class="text-accent font-medium hover:underline"
            >
              waterproof overtrousers
            </a>{" "}
            <em>over</em> the outside of the boot collar (using stirrup straps)
            and pair with merino hiking socks.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 1: Gore-Tex vs Brand Membranes -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">
          1. Gore-Tex vs. Brand-Own Waterproof Membranes
        </h2>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          When shopping for kids' boots, you will usually see either{" "}
          <strong class="text-foreground">GORE-TEX</strong> or a brand's own
          proprietary membrane (such as{" "}
          <strong class="text-foreground">Keen.Dry</strong>,{" "}
          <strong class="text-foreground">ReimaTec</strong>, or{" "}
          <strong class="text-foreground">Salomon ClimaSalomon</strong>).
        </p>
        <ul class="text-muted-foreground mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">GORE-TEX:</strong> The gold
            standard for durability and strict factory seam-sealing inspections.
            Adds £15–£25 to the price tag, but holds up reliably across rough
            scrambling.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Proprietary Membranes:</strong>{" "}
            Modern brand-own liners use nearly identical microporous technology
            at a lower price point. For kids who outgrow footwear every 6 to 9
            months, proprietary membranes offer fantastic value—provided the
            boot has a <strong class="text-foreground">gusseted tongue</strong>{" "}
            (sewn to the sides of the boot so water can't sneak through the
            laces).
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Why Boots Fail in Wet Grass -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">
          2. Four Reasons "Waterproof" Boots Fail in Wet Grass
        </h2>
        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
          {
            leakReasons.map((item) => (
              <div class="border-border bg-card/50 rounded-xl border p-5">
                <h3 class="font-bold">{item.title}</h3>
                <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed">
                  {item.notes}
                </p>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: How to Keep Feet Dry -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">
          3. How to Actually Keep Kids' Feet Dry in Scottish Weather
        </h2>
        <ul class="text-muted-foreground mt-3 list-disc space-y-2.5 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Use boot stirrups on waterproof trousers:</strong>{" "}
            Never tuck waterproof trousers inside boots—that funnels rainwater
            straight onto their socks. Pull cuffs over the ankle and hook the
            elastic stirrup under the sole.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Check the tongue height:</strong>{" "}
            A boot is only waterproof up to the lowest point where the tongue
            connects to the upper. Look for a fully gusseted tongue sewn high up
            the ankle.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">Brush off mud &amp; reproof:</strong>{" "}
            Once muddy boots dry, brush them off and apply a spray-on footwear
            DWR treatment so water beads off the leather or mesh instead of
            soaking in.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Related Guides -->
      <aside class="bg-muted/50 border-border rounded-xl border p-6">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold">Continue Exploring Gear Guides</h2>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm">
          <li>
            &rarr; Pair your boots with the right socks:{" "}
            <a
              href="/gear-explained/hiking-socks-explained"
              class="text-accent font-medium hover:underline"
            >
              Kids' Hiking Socks Explained
            </a>
            .
          </li>
          <li>
            &rarr; Learn how to restore water beading on outerwear and boots in
            our{" "}
            <a
              href="/gear-explained/gear-care"
              class="text-accent font-medium hover:underline"
            >
              Gear Care Hub
            </a>
            .
          </li>
        </ul>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>